If you're inside the PostgreSQL prompt (psql), after running:

CREATE DATABASE vegetable_db;

CREATE USER veguser
WITH PASSWORD 'StrongPassword';

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES
ON DATABASE vegetable_db
TO veguser;

you don't need to "save" anything manually. PostgreSQL executes each command immediately when it ends with a semicolon (;).

To exit psql

Type:

\q

and press Enter.

Example
ubuntu@ip-10-0-1-100:~$ sudo -u postgres psql
postgres=# CREATE DATABASE vegetable_db;
CREATE DATABASE

postgres=# CREATE USER veguser
postgres-# WITH PASSWORD 'StrongPassword';
CREATE ROLE

postgres=# GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES
postgres-# ON DATABASE vegetable_db
postgres-# TO veguser;
GRANT

postgres=# \q

You'll return to the Linux shell:

ubuntu@ip-10-0-1-100:~$
Verify the database and user

Log back into PostgreSQL:

sudo -u postgres psql

List databases:

\l

You should see:

vegetable_db

List users (roles):

\du

You should see:

veguser
